Creational

1. Singleton (Spring beans)

In Spring Boot, classes annotated with @Service, @Component, @Repository, or @Controller are singletons by default (only one instance per application context).

In your project:

TripService.java, JwtUtil.java, EmailService.java → Spring creates and reuses a single instance for each, avoiding multiple object creations.

Structural

2. MVC (Model–View–Controller)

You’ve structured your project into layers:

Controllers (e.g., TripController, DeliveryController) handle HTTP requests.

Models/Entities (User, Trip, etc. in model package) represent database tables.

Views (your React frontend in src/views) present the UI.

This keeps separation of concerns clear.

3. Repository (DAO Pattern)

You’re using Spring Data JPA repositories like TripRepository.java and UserRepository.java.

They abstract CRUD operations without you writing SQL directly.

Example: tripRepository.findByOriginAndDestination(...) → encapsulates database queries.

4. DTO (Data Transfer Object)

You’ve created DTOs to shape data before sending it to the client.

Example: TripWithDistanceDTO.java → carries trip details plus computed distance instead of exposing raw entity.

5. Adapter

Example: JwtUtil.java wraps a third-party JWT library.

Instead of exposing the external API directly, you provide clean methods like generateToken() and validateToken().

This adapts external functionality to your project’s needs.

6. Facade

Controllers (TripController, DeliveryController) act as facades.

They expose a simple REST API (/api/trips, /api/delivery) and delegate complex business logic to services.

Clients (React frontend / Postman) only interact with the facade (controller), not the internals.

7. Chain of Responsibility (via Filters)

Example: JwtFilter.java.

It intercepts requests before they reach controllers.

Requests pass through a filter chain (FilterChain.doFilter()), where each filter decides whether to handle or pass it along.

You use it for authentication checks before controllers execute.

Behavioral

8. Chain of Responsibility (again)

Same as above, but here you’re applying the behavioral intent of the pattern: different handlers (filters) in a sequence, each can either process or pass forward.

Cross-cutting / Architectural

9. Layered Architecture / Service Layer

You’ve cleanly separated layers:

Controller → Service → Repository.

Example:

TripController → receives request.

Calls TripService → business logic (like calculating distance).

TripService calls TripRepository → persistence.

This is the Service Layer pattern.

10. RESTful API Style

Controllers (/api/trips, /api/users) expose REST endpoints returning JSON.

Follows REST principles (resources, verbs: GET/POST/PUT/DELETE).

11. Caching

In TripService, you mentioned @Cacheable usage for trip-search.

This is a caching pattern where repeated queries are stored in cache (like Redis or in-memory) to avoid hitting DB every time.

✅ So in summary, you used:

Singleton (Spring beans)

MVC (Controller/Model/View separation)

Repository (DAO)

DTO

Adapter (JwtUtil wrapper)

Facade (controllers exposing simplified APIs)

Chain of Responsibility (filters in request pipeline)

Layered Architecture / Service Layer

RESTful API

Caching
